## Remediation-plan audit and executable sequencing

Reviewed the three-epic / 33-issue remediation plan against the agreed evaluation contract and corpus measurements, then tightened the dependency graph.

Added:

- KDATAP-471fdc — component entity identity and same-subtype cardinality, after measuring 53 duplicate repo/key groups covering 456 accepted component cases;
- KDATAP-b87baf — machine-enforced baseline readiness gate separating migration audits from citable series 1;
- KDATAP-5cef81 — introduction of the temporary compatibility loader, split from KDATAP-f009ab removal so migration dependencies run in the correct direction.

Corrected the plan so:

- declared capability coverage is diagnostic only and never replaces raw recall;
- source-token-only gold is migration-incomplete, not a permanent detector miss;
- legacy subject names are preserved as candidates and adjudicated, not blindly promoted or used for identity;
- flow gold is unscorable only in migration audits; the first four-layer baseline waits for canonical flow readiness;
- per-layer eligibility uses structured outcomes, including distinct capacity causes, and does not infer state from warnings;
- same-subtype component entities retain cardinality without making display names mandatory identity;
- temporary legacy compatibility is introduced before migration and removed after all migrations, docs, and readiness validation.

The resulting order is: settle remaining design rulings -> canonical IR and temporary loader -> adapters, eligibility ledger, and structured graph model -> human gold migrations and one-to-one evaluator -> four-layer runner and docs -> readiness gate -> remove compatibility -> freeze baseline series 1.

Kanbus validation passes with canonical dependency targets. Scanner retuning and new language ingest remain outside this plan.
